%[text] ## Simple Linear Regression
%[text] Suppose you have collected data on how the temperature of a metal rod, the dependent variable, changes over time when exposed to a heat source, the independent variable. To analyze this data to understand the relationship between time and temperature and make predictions, you can apply regression analysis to model and interpret the data.
%[text]{"align":"center"} .![Metal Rod](text:image:4a03)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_43e2] [Simple linear regression](internal:M_2320) is the ideal statistical method for modeling the linear relationship between temperature and time, as it captures the linear relationship between one dependent variable and one independent variable. It is called *simple* because it involves only one predictor, the time the rod is exposed to heat in our example, and *linear* because the relationship is assumed to be a straight line. The straight line is represented by the equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y=\\beta\_1x+\\beta\_0${"altText":"y=\beta\_1x+\beta\_0"}       ($\\star${"altText":"\star"})
%[text] where 
%[text] - $y${"altText":"y"} is the dependent variable
%[text] - $x${"altText":"x"} is the independent variable
%[text] - $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} is the y-intercept
%[text] - $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"} is the slope \
%[text] *Regression* is a statistical technique used to estimate the relationship that best captures the relationship between inputs and outputs, so we can predict future values of $y${"altText":"y"} given new values of $x${"altText":"x"}.
%[text] The goal of [simple linear regression](internal:M_2320) is to find a function that best fits the data and can be used to predict future values of the dependent variable based on new inputs. There are infinitely many lines we must consider in search of the best fit. The tools of linear regression will help you make an optimal choice quickly. Before we consider the mathematics, let's first consider a small selection of possibilities. 
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:51c6) **Try**. Find the line that best captures the data in the scatter plot.
%[text] 1. Click **Display Data**.
%[text] 2. Check the box(es) to choose the fit(s) you want to display. \
  %[control:button:424b]{"position":[1,2]}
load SampleData.mat x y % Load data
Fit = [false,false,false,false,false,false]; %[control:checkbox:899b]{"position":[14,19]} %[control:checkbox:6587]{"position":[20,25]} %[control:checkbox:2824]{"position":[26,31]} %[control:checkbox:19bd]{"position":[32,37]} %[control:checkbox:90b9]{"position":[38,43]}
T = compareFits(x,y,Fit,1,0); % Call a function to plot data and display best fits.
%[text] ![Lightbulb Icon](text:image:867e) **Reflect**. Which best-fit line do you think best shows the overall pattern in the data, and why?
%%
%[text] ### **Perform Least-Squares Regression to Find "Best" Fit Line**
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_7fb6] [Least squares regression](internal:M_11b8) is a method for finding a function, usually a line, that best fits a set of data points. It does this by minimizing the total squared difference between the actual values. The residuals are squared to ensure the errors don't cancel out, and larger errors are penalized more heavily. The result of **least squares regression** is a line that balances all the data points as closely as possible, giving us a regression model we can use to make predictions. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_6311] The least squares line is also known as the line of "**best" fit** or the [regression line](internal:M_9735)**,** $y${"altText":"y"}, which is given mathematically as: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_7b36] $\\hat{y}=\\beta\_1x+\\beta\_0${"altText":"\hat{y}=\beta\_1x+\beta\_0"}.    ( $\\star${"altText":"\star"}$\\star${"altText":"\star"})
%[text] To help us distinguish between the actual value,$y${"altText":"y"}, and the predicted value, we add a "hat" to obtain the $\\hat{y}${"altText":"\hat{y}"}.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_7986] The [residual](internal:M_04f8) is the difference between the observed value $y${"altText":"y"} and the predicted value $\\hat{y}${"altText":"\hat{y}"}. The residual is represented on a plot as the vertical distance between each data point and the regression line, and is mathematically expressed as the difference between the observed value and the predicted value.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Predicted}=y-\\hat{y}${"altText":"\text{Residual}=\text{Actual}-\text{Predicted}=y-\hat{y}"}. 
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:32e5) **Try**. **Select a fit** from the dropdown menu and then click **Visualize the Residuals**.
figure
load SampleData.mat x y % Load data
[yEquations,FitOptions] = SetEquations(x); %#ok<ASGLU>
Fit = yEquations(1); %[control:dropdown:2660]{"position":[7,20]}
if ~strcmp(Fit,"Select") && ~isequal(Fit,yEquations(1))
      %[control:button:1a8c]{"position":[5,6]}
    T = compareFits(x,y,Fit,1,0); %#ok<NASGU> % Call a function to plot data,displays best fits.
else
    warning("Select a valid option.")
    return
end
%[text] ![Lightbulb Icon](text:image:06a5) **Reflect**. What do you notice about the residuals? How do they help you determine which prediction is better than another?
%%
%[text] ### Sum of Squared Errors
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_6540] To calculate the total error in a model, we would like to sum all the individual errors. However, if we simply add up residuals, we can end up with a small or even zero value when positive and negative errors cancel each other out, giving the impression of a better prediction than the model actually provides. By squaring the residuals before summing them, we prevent positive and negative values from offsetting one another. The sum of squared residuals can only be zero when no error is present at all. For that reason, the [sum of squared errors (SSE](internal:M_30ba)**)** is used to measure the total deviation between the actual values and those predicted by the model.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $SSE=\\sum\_{i=1}^{n}{(y\_i-(\\beta\_0+\\beta\_1x\_i))^2=\\sum\_{i=1}^{n}{(y\_i-\\hat{y\_i})^2${"altText":"SSE=\sum\_{i=1}^{n}{(y\_i-(\beta\_0+\beta\_1x\_i))^2=\sum\_{i=1}^{n}{(y\_i-\hat{y\_i})^2"}
%[text] where $y\_i${"altText":"y\_i"} is the actual value of the dependent variable corresponding to the $i^\\text{th}${"altText":"i^\text{th}"} data sample out of $n${"altText":"n"} total samples, and $\\beta\_1x+\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_1x+\beta\_0"}, or $\\hat{y\_i}${"altText":"\hat{y\_i}"} is the predicted value corresponding to the $i^\\text{th}${"altText":"i^\text{th}"} data sample out of $n${"altText":"n"} total samples.
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:9dc7) **Try**. Review the **SSE** table and the graph to decide which model fits best.    
%[text] 1. **Select a fit** from the dropdown menu.
%[text] 2. Click **View SSE table and the Corresponding Best Fit.** \
figure
load SampleData.mat x y % Load data
[yEquations,FitOptions] = SetEquations(x);
Fit = yEquations(1); %[control:dropdown:5ded]{"position":[7,20]}
  %[control:button:43fe]{"position":[1,2]}
T = compareFits(x,y,Fit,1,1); % Call a function to plot data,displays best fits.
%%
%[text]  ![](text:image:3f16) **Exercise 1**. Which fit is the best fit based on the **SSE** value?
if exist("yEquations","var")
    option = yEquations(1); %[control:dropdown:245a]{"position":[14,27]}
      %[control:button:4744]{"position":[5,6]}
    option = string(option);
    CheckAnswer("Exercise1",option); % Call a function to check the answer.
else
    warning("Select a best fit option from above.")
end
%%
%[text] ### Use Simple Linear Regression to Estimate a Home's Value
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of rowa  of different-sized houses](text:image:94ab)
%[text] ![Earth icon](text:image:6c62) **Application.** Imagine you're estimating a home's value based on its square footage using data from recent sales in your neighborhood. While larger homes generally sell for more, the relationship isn't perfectly linear, as some large homes sell for less, and some smaller ones for more. To capture this trend, you can use simple linear regression, which models the relationship between the independent variable, the square footage, and the dependent variable, the price, with a straight line. The goal is to determine the line's coefficients so that the error between predicted and actual prices is minimized, helping you better understand how square footage influences home value.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  ![A scatter plot of house price vs. area.](text:image:908e)
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:375c) **Try**. Find the Regression Line for the data plotted above.
%[text] 1. Click **Plot the Regression Line**.
%[text] 2. Adjust the $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"} and $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} values, where $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"} is the slope, and $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} is the y-intercept, to find an approximate straight line to model or fit the data. \
load LinearData.mat x y
B1 = "Select";% Set value for coefficient b1 %[control:dropdown:25cf]{"position":[6,14]}
B0 =  "Select";% Set value for coefficient b0 %[control:dropdown:3ac6]{"position":[7,15]}
  %[control:button:1095]{"position":[1,2]}
if strcmp(B0,"Select") || strcmp(B1,"Select")
    CreateStruct.Interpreter = "latex";
    CreateStruct.WindowStyle = "modal";
    syms beta_0 beta_1
    displayFormula(['"Select a value for "' "beta_0" '" and "' "beta_1" '"."']);
    return
else
    figure
    hScatterHouse = ScatterPlot(x,y);
    hold on
    FitHouse = B1.*x + B0;
    [~,~] = RegressFit(x,y,FitHouse);
    legend(["Data","Regression Line"],Location="northwest")
    hold off

    SSEHouse = round(sum((y - FitHouse).^2));
    SSEHouseSci = sprintf("%.3e",SSEHouse);
    xlabel("Total Living Area(sqft.)",Interpreter="latex")
    ylabel("House Price(USD)",Interpreter="latex")
    title("The sum of the squared errors (SSE) is: " + SSEHouseSci)
end
%[text] ![Lightbulb Icon](text:image:29d7) **Reflect**. How confident are you that you found the best fit?
%%
%[text] ### Analytical Solution for Simple Linear Regression
%[text] Earlier, we focused on minimizing error visually. This meant creating a plot, looking at it, deciding on a new set of parameters to try, and repeating the process, which is not an efficient approach. Instead, we can use calculus to minimize the **SSE** and directly solve for the regression line coefficients $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} and $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"}. These coefficients define the slope and intercept of the line that best fits the data. Solving for them defines a model that minimizes the total squared differences between actual and predicted values. This process is at the heart of regression.
%[text] By substituting the linear model $y=\\beta\_1\*x+\\beta\_0${"altText":"y=\beta\_1\*x+\beta\_0"}, we can express  [simple linear regression](internal:M_2320) as the optimization problem of minimizing the total squared differences between the observed values and the predicted values: 
%[text] The equations are shown below:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\underset{\\beta\_0,\\beta\_1}{\\min} \\sum\_{i=1}^{n} \\left( y\_i - (\\beta\_1 x\_i+\\beta\_0 ) \\right)^2${"altText":"\underset{\beta\_0,\beta\_1}{\min} \sum\_{i=1}^{n} \left( y\_i - (\beta\_1 x\_i+\beta\_0 ) \right)^2"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_1b51] You can solve this analytically for $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"} and $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} to get: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_7a57]  $\\( \n\\beta\_1 = \\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^{n} (x\_i - \\bar{x})(y\_i - \\bar{y})}{\\sum\_{i=1}^{n} (x\_i - \\bar{x})^2} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n\\beta\_0 = \\bar{y} - \\beta\_1 \\bar{x} \\qquad\n\n(\\star\\star\\star)${"altText":"(  \beta\_1 = \frac{\sum\_{i=1}^{n} (x\_i - \bar{x})(y\_i - \bar{y})}{\sum\_{i=1}^{n} (x\_i - \bar{x})^2} \qquad \text{and} \qquad \beta\_0 = \bar{y} - \beta\_1 \bar{x} \qquad  (\star\star\star)"}
%[text] where 
%[text] $\\bar{x}${"altText":"\bar{x}"} (xbar) is the average of all $x\_i${"altText":"x\_i"}
%[text] $\\bar{y}${"altText":"\bar{y}"} (ybar) is the average of all $y\_i${"altText":"y\_i"}
%[text] $i${"altText":"i"} indicates the individual data samples from a total of $n${"editStyle":"visual"} samples
%[text] Access a full [proof of ordinary least squares for simple linear regression](https://statproofbook.github.io/P/slr-ols.html#:~:text=RSS(%CE%B20,x2i)(5)) used to arrive at the equations for $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} and $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"}.
%%
%[text] ### Evaluate SSE: Guess vs. Optimal Regression
%[text] In this section, we compare your self‑selected “guess” for the regression line with the analytical solution derived from the equations above ([ $\\star${"altText":"\star"}$\\star${"altText":"\star"}$\\star${"altText":"\star"}](internal:M_7a57)) using the following MATLAB code:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] beta1 = sum((x-xbar).*(y-ybar))/sum((x-xbar).^2) % Calculation for beta1 from the analytical solution
%[text] beta0 = ybar - beta1*xbar % Calculation for beta0 from the analytical solution
%[text] % Note: The .* and .^ operators perform element-wise array operations.
%[text] ```
%[text] We calculate the **SSE** for both your guess and the true coefficients, then evaluate how far your guess deviates from the optimal values to illustrate how least squares identifies the best-fit line.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Scatter plot of x vs. y.](text:image:1c35)
%[text]  ![](text:image:01c1) **Exercise 2**. Test different values of $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} and $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"}to see how they change the fit and the **SSE.** Try to get the best fit possible. Recall, the equation for the regression line: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y = \\beta\_1 x + \\beta\_0${"altText":"y = \beta\_1 x + \beta\_0"}.
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:6d54) **Try**. Select values for $\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"} and $\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"} below and then click **Validate Answer**.
load SampleData.mat x y
b1Guess ="Select"; %[control:dropdown:2af5]{"position":[10,18]}
b0Guess =  "Select"; %[control:dropdown:040f]{"position":[12,20]}
if ~strcmp("Select",b1Guess) && ~strcmp("Select",b0Guess)
    evaluateLinearFit(x,y,b1Guess,b0Guess)
else
    warning("Select valid options for b0 and b1.")
    return
end
  %[control:button:7e17]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] ![Lightbulb Icon](text:image:711f) **Reflect**. If you found the optimal fit for exercise 2, how did the SSE for that fit compare to the other models?
%%
%[text] ### Use the Regression Model to Predict Prices
%[text] Now that we have the coefficients ($\\beta\_0${"altText":"\beta\_0"},$\\beta\_1${"altText":"\beta\_1"}), we can substitute them into our regression equation to predict future values. For example, our dataset includes homes up to 6,000 square feet. While we have prices for some square footage, others are missing—this is why we use a regression line: to estimate values where data is incomplete.
%[text] Before making predictions, keep these cautions in mind:
%[text] - **Stay within range**: Predict only for square footage within the range of your original data.
%[text] - **Visualize the fit**: Plot the data and the regression line to check how well it represents the trend.
%[text] - **Watch for outliers**: Extreme values can distort the regression line and lead to inaccurate predictions. \
%[text] Let’s apply these cautions to make our predictions with our best-fit line. Suppose we want to predict the price of a house with 3,470 square feet. Here, square footage is the independent variable, and price is the dependent variable. Using our regression equation ( [$\\star${"altText":"\star"}$\\star${"altText":"\star"}](internal:M_7b36)), we can calculate the estimated price.
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:4e6b) **Try**. Estimate the price of a 3,470 square foot home. 
  %[control:button:6c7f]{"position":[1,2]}
load LinearData.mat x y
[xData,yData] = prepareCurveData(x,y);

[fitresult,~] = fit(xData,yData,"poly1");

b0 = fitresult.p2;
b1 = fitresult.p1;

SqFt= 0; %[control:editfield:6811]{"position":[7,8]}


Price = b1*SqFt + b0;
Price = round(Price,2);

PriceStr = string(num2str(Price,'%.2f'));
PriceStr = regexprep(PriceStr,'\d(?=(\d{3})+\.)','$0,');

SqFtStr = string(num2str(SqFt));
SqFtStr = regexprep(SqFtStr,'\d(?=(\d{3})+$)','$0,');

disp("The estimated price for " + SqFtStr + " sqft. is $" + PriceStr + ".")


% Plot
h = plot(fitresult,xData,yData);
legend(h,"Data","Regression Line",Location="best");

hold on
scatter(SqFt,Price,"d","filled",DisplayName="Estimated Price for " + SqFtStr + " sqft.");

xlabel("Total Living Area (sqft.)",Interpreter="latex")
ylabel("House Price (USD)",Interpreter="latex")
hold off
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7db0) **Exercise 3.** Use the section above to predict the price of a 1,864 sqft. home.
CheckAnswer("Exercise3","Select") %[control:dropdown:20a9]{"position":[25,33]}
  %[control:button:7f24]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ## Interpreting Regression Model Performance: 95% Confidence Interval
%[text] Having identified the best fit line, we can deepen our understanding of the model by introducing the 95% confidence interval, which provides a measure of uncertainty around our estimates. We will review this before you try applying regression on your own. Residual plots reveal errors and patterns but not uncertainty around the regression line. The **95% confidence interval** addresses this by showing where the true line likely falls, offering a clear measure of reliability. On a scatter plot, it appears as a shaded band:
%[text] - narrow bands indicate precise predictions and strong features
%[text] - wide or uneven bands suggest noise or poor feature selection.  \
%[text] ![Finger touching surface icon](text:image:6651) **Try**. Visualize a narrow and a wide 95% confidence interval by clicking **View Different Confidence Intervals**.
ConfInt;
  %[control:button:179e]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Try Linear Regression on Your Own
%[text] In this script, we have described the concepts and underpinnings of simple linear regression. Learn how to import a dataset in MATLAB, build a regression model for a lung ventilator, and use that model to predict future values in [Practice Linear Regression](file:./TryRegression.m).
